---
import BmiCalculator from '@/components/calculators/BmiCalculator';
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is a healthy BMI?',
    a: 'For adults, the World Health Organization defines 18.5–24.9 as the healthy range, 25–29.9 as overweight and 30 or above as obesity. Health risk rises gradually, not at a cliff edge.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is BMI accurate?',
    a: 'At population level it tracks health risk well. For an individual it can mislead: muscular people read as overweight, and older adults can read as healthy while carrying excess fat. Pair it with waist circumference or a body-fat estimate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does BMI differ by ethnicity?',
    a: 'Yes. Health risks appear at lower BMIs in people of South Asian, Chinese and some other backgrounds; guidelines in the UK and elsewhere use 23 and 27.5 as the thresholds for those groups.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the ponderal index?',
    a: 'Weight divided by height cubed. It is fairer than BMI for very tall or very short people, whose BMI is skewed by the square-of-height formula. A healthy range is roughly 11–15 kg/m³.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout
  slug="bmi"
  title="BMI Calculator"
  description="Free BMI calculator with the WHO categories, your healthy weight range and how far you are from it. Metric and imperial, instant results."
  lede="Calculate your body mass index, see where it sits on the WHO scale and get the healthy weight range for your height. Then read why it is only a starting point."
  faq={faq}
>
  <BmiCalculator client:load slot="calculator" />

  <h2>How BMI is calculated</h2>
  <p>
    BMI is your weight in kilograms divided by your height in metres squared. A 70 kg person who is
    1.75 m tall has a BMI of 70 ÷ (1.75 × 1.75) = 22.9. It was devised in the 1830s as a statistical
    tool and became a clinical screening measure because it is free, fast and correlates with health
    outcomes across large groups.
  </p>
  <h2>What it cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    BMI does not know how much of your weight is muscle, where your fat is stored, or how fit you
    are. Two people with the same BMI can have completely different health profiles. If your BMI is
    above 25, the most useful next step is a <a href="/tools/body-fat/">body-fat estimate</a> and a
    waist measurement: a waist of more than half your height is a stronger warning sign than BMI
    alone.
  </p>
  <h2>Using it well</h2>
  <p>
    Use the healthy weight range as a broad guide, then set a goal that reflects your build and your
    body-fat percentage rather than the arithmetic midpoint. When you are ready, the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/calories/">calorie calculator</a> will show you how to get there.
  </p>
</ToolLayout>
